    },
    {
      "text": "[CLAIM: Anthropic says] Glasswing partners found at least 129,000 verified vulnerabilities, and Anthropic is using this to justify widening access to many more defenders. Only Anthropic's own data supports this, and it says the data is partial.",
      "ids": [
        "F11",
        "F12"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    {
      "id": "F1",
      "text": "Anthropic launched an expanded Cyber Verification Program (CVP) that gives qualifying security professionals advanced cyber capabilities and reduced blocking classifiers, now in three access tiers.",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "SiliconANGLE"
      ],
      "claim_by": null,
      "notes": [
        "Announcement is Anthropic's own; SiliconANGLE confirms the announcement and describes it but adds no independent verification."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F2",
      "text": "Every tier includes Claude Opus 5.5, Claude Sonnet 5.5, Claude Mythos 5.1 and new models going forward.",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "SiliconANGLE"
      ],
      "claim_by": null,
      "notes": []
    },
    {
      "id": "F3",
      "text": "Defense Access covers defensive work (security operations center and incident response, malware reverse-engineering, vulnerability analysis and validation). Eligible applicants include company, nonprofit, university and government security teams defending systems they own or maintain, critical infrastructure operators of any size (e.g. regional hospitals, municipal utilities), smaller security firms, open-source maintainers, and individual researchers with a track record of reported vulnerabilities. Anthropic aims to respond within a few days.",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "SiliconANGLE"
      ],
      "claim_by": null,
      "notes": []
    },
    {
      "id": "F4",
      "text": "Red Team Access adds authorized penetration testing and red-teaming against systems the organization is authorized to test; real-time blocks remain on actions that could cause physical harm or mass disruption (e.g. deploying ransomware, damaging physical systems, pen testing high-risk safety systems). Review takes a few weeks, applicants are enrolled in Defense Access meanwhile, and individual researchers are not currently eligible.",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "SiliconANGLE"
      ],
      "claim_by": null,
      "notes": []
    },
    {
      "id": "F5",
      "text": "Specialized Access, the tier with the fewest cyber blocks, is for a limited set of verified organizations authorized to test safety systems that could affect lives or disrupt markets (flight operating systems, power grids, telecom networks, interbank transfer infrastructure, government administrative networks); every organization is reviewed in depth with the US government, and existing Project Glasswing members move into it without reapproval for current models.",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "SiliconANGLE"
      ],
      "claim_by": null,
      "notes": []
    },
    {
      "id": "F6",
      "text": "Anthropic says cybersecurity is dual use, so its generally available models (Claude Opus 5.5, Claude Fable 5.1, Claude Sonnet 5.5) have conservative cyber safeguards that block most cyber work, while still allowing tasks such as code review, patching known issues, vulnerability finding in owned source code and alert triage.",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "SiliconANGLE"
      ],
      "claim_by": "Anthropic",
      "notes": [
        "SiliconANGLE repeats the dual-use framing and says most security tasks sent to Opus 5.5 at launch (Sept. 22) were routed to the older Opus 4.8 (SiliconANGLE only)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F7",
      "text": "Organizations in the program must allow data retention so Anthropic can monitor for cyber misuse; Enterprise Frontier Safeguards (EFS), due later this fall, will let eligible organizations store data in cloud infrastructure they control. Until then, organizations with Claude Fable 5.1 or Mythos 5.1 zero-data-retention access can use CVP with zero data retention.",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "SiliconANGLE"
      ],
      "claim_by": null,
      "notes": [
        "SiliconANGLE says EFS is 'due later this year'."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F8",
      "text": "CVP is available on the Claude Platform, Google Cloud's Vertex AI and Microsoft Foundry; on Amazon Bedrock it is only for customers eligible for Enterprise Frontier Safeguards.",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "SiliconANGLE"
      ],
      "claim_by": null,
      "notes": []
    },
    {
      "id": "F9",
      "text": "Existing CVP members keep their current settings for previous models and will be automatically evaluated for access to Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5 and Mythos 5.1; Anthropic will verify all applicants and request proof of required security controls for the relevant tier.",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic"
      ],
      "claim_by": null,
      "notes": []
    },
    {
      "id": "F10",
      "text": "[CLAIM: Anthropic says] In its CyScenarioBench test (10 challenges, five attempts each, per tier, with Claude Opus 5.5), every task without CVP access was blocked on the first prompt; Defense Access blocked 46 of 50 trials at some point (four succeeded); Red Team Access had no blocks and 34 of 50 tasks completed, which Anthropic calls effectively equivalent to the model's 67.6% success rate with no safeguards (representative of Specialized Access).",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "SiliconANGLE"
      ],
      "claim_by": "Anthropic",
      "notes": [
        "Anthropic's own evaluation; no independent verification found. SiliconANGLE only restates Anthropic's results.",
        "34 of 50 is 68%; Anthropic compares it to 67.6% and calls it 'effectively equivalent'."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F11",
      "text": "[CLAIM: Anthropic says] Through Project Glasswing, partners uncovered at least 129,000 verified software vulnerabilities between April and July 2026, and Anthropic's own open-source scanning found an additional 5,500 between April and October 2026; more than 33,000 of these verified vulnerabilities have so far been rated critical- or high-severity.",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "SiliconANGLE"
      ],
      "claim_by": "Anthropic",
      "notes": [
        "No independent confirmation found; SiliconANGLE restates the figures. Anthropic's post says 'of these'; SiliconANGLE says 'of that combined total'."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F12",
      "text": "[CLAIM: Anthropic says] The vulnerability figures are likely an undercount: they rely on 33 partner reports and Anthropic's open-source partnerships, fewer than 50% of partners disclosed patched numbers, and Anthropic expects true impact to be at least five times higher; several partners said the models sped up finding vulnerabilities by months or even years.",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "SiliconANGLE"
      ],
      "claim_by": "Anthropic",
      "notes": [
        "SiliconANGLE repeats the 33 reports, fewer-than-half patch disclosure and 'at least five times higher' claims, attributed to the company."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "background": [
    {
      "id": "B1",
      "text": "For the past six months Anthropic gave trusted cyber access through two programs: Project Glasswing (Claude Mythos access for organizations securing the most critical software) and the CVP (reduced safeguards on Claude Opus and Sonnet for vetted security teams); the new program merges them.",
